    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "but that \"text and history\" test seems like a total loophole for the court to just ignore modern reality. if a law can't be traced back to the 1700s, it basically doesn't count? doesn't that just make any modern safety regulation effectively illegal because the founders couldn't have imagined modern semi-automatic weapons?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"text and history\" test does not exclude modern weapons, but rather requires that the manner of regulation bear some connection to the historical justifications for the right they address. However, critics argue that because the Second Amendment text and the 18th-century consensus explicitly protected \"military\" and \"lawful\" uses over \"civilian\" ones, the Court may now deem burdensome modern gun controls as unconstitutional if they lack a direct historical counterpart in the 18th century. This framework creates genuine legal uncertainty, as it effectively invalidates modern safety measures (like assault weapon bans or universal background checks) if proponents cannot demonstrate how these specific prohibitions were justified in 1791.",
